# ── SECURITY PROGRAMME MANAGEMENT ────────────────────────────────────────────
("How do you build a cybersecurity programme from scratch at a company that has nothing?", "Building a security programme from zero: Month 1 (Know your environment): asset inventory -- enumerate everything (cloud accounts, SaaS applications, endpoints, servers, network devices); data classification -- where is your most sensitive data? Who has access? Identity audit -- list all accounts, admin accounts, shared accounts, service accounts; MFA gap analysis. Month 2-3 (Stop the bleeding -- quick wins): deploy MFA for email and remote access (highest ROI control, stops credential attacks); remove unnecessary admin accounts; ensure all internet-facing systems are patched (focus on Critical CVEs); configure automatic updates for endpoints; enable cloud security defaults (Entra security defaults, AWS Security Hub). Month 3-6 (Foundation): deploy EDR on all endpoints; centralised logging (cloud-native SIEM: Microsoft Sentinel, Chronicle, or Splunk Cloud); basic IR playbooks; security awareness training + phishing simulation; vulnerability scanner deployed; patch management process formalised. Month 6-12 (Structure): risk register populated; security policies written; RBAC implemented across major systems; vendor security assessment programme; penetration test (external); incident response plan tested (tabletop exercise). Year 2 (Maturity): SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001 readiness; threat hunting programme; full coverage monitoring (network, cloud, endpoint, identity); purple team exercises; security metrics dashboard for board. Budget: prioritise MFA > EDR > logging > pen test > everything else. Show ROI: cyber insurance reduction, audit findings closed, incident count trends."),
("What makes a great CISO and how do they operate strategically?", "Effective CISO attributes and operating model: (1) Business acumen -- understand the company's revenue model, customer obligations, regulatory environment, and competitive pressures; security decisions must align with business strategy, not oppose it; 'the business of security is the business.' (2) Risk communication -- translate technical risk into financial and operational language for the board; run risk as a business discipline with quantified decisions, not a compliance exercise. (3) Relationship capital -- CISO effectiveness depends on trust with: CEO (strategic alignment), CFO (budget), CTO/CIO (technical partnership), Legal (regulatory), HR (insider threat, culture), business unit leaders (they own the risk). Build relationships before crises occur. (4) Operating model -- define what the security team owns vs partners with IT, engineering, and legal; write a security charter; avoid being a bottleneck (shift-left, enabling teams to be secure independently). (5) Strategic planning -- 3-year security roadmap aligned to business growth plan; annual plan with quarterly OKRs; tie security maturity milestones to business milestones (SOC 2 before enterprise sales motion, HIPAA before healthcare market entry). (6) Board engagement -- attend board meetings at least quarterly; present using the 'risk dial' concept (how exposed are we vs last quarter and why); never present without a specific ask (budget, policy decision, risk acceptance). (7) Talent strategy -- build vs buy vs partner; security talent is scarce; culture and mission attract talent more than salary at the margins; invest in internal development. (8) Continuous learning -- threat landscape changes constantly; read Mandiant/CrowdStrike annual threat reports; engage with ISAC (Information Sharing and Analysis Centre) for sector-specific intel."),

# ── ADVANCED BINARY EXPLOITATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────
("Explain heap exploitation using tcache poisoning in glibc 2.32+.", "Tcache (thread-local cache) was introduced in glibc 2.26 as a per-thread free list for small allocations. In glibc 2.28+, tcache entries have a key field (a pointer to the tcache_perthread_struct) to detect double-free. In glibc 2.32+, tcache next pointer is mangled (XOR with heap address shifted right 12 bits) to prevent direct pointer overwrite. Exploit with safe-linking bypass: when reading the next pointer of a freed chunk, unmangle it: `mangle = (pos >> 12) ^ ptr` where pos is the address of the chunk. To overwrite: control an allocation that overwrites the mangled next pointer of a freed tcache chunk with `(attacker_target >> 12) ^ (heap_base >> 12)`. Two allocations then yield a chunk at attacker_target. Technique to leak heap base: the first tcache chunk's next pointer is NULL, stored as `(chunk_addr >> 12) ^ 0 = chunk_addr >> 12`; partial overwrite the LSB to redirect to a nearby heap address; free and allocate to obtain a chunk at the leaked region, revealing the heap base. Exploit flow: double-free a chunk (bypass key check by zero-ing the key field via heap overflow or UAF write); overwrite mangled next with target address (with correct mangling using leaked heap base); allocate twice; second allocation is at target (e.g., __malloc_hook or a libc global, or a GOT entry). __malloc_hook was removed in glibc 2.34; post-2.34 targets: __libc_IO_vtables, environ (libc pointer to stack address for ROP)."),
("How does kernel exploitation differ from userland exploitation?", "Kernel exploitation targets the operating system kernel (ring 0). Key differences from userland: (1) Primitives are different -- kernel memory is shared across all processes; a read primitive leaks kernel addresses (defeating KASLR); a write primitive can overwrite any data structure. Common targets: cred struct (uid/gid for the current process, overwrite to 0 = root), modprobe_path (path of binary executed as root when unknown file format is executed -- overwrite with /tmp/evil), function pointers in kernel structures (overwrite with ROP gadget or shellcode). (2) KASLR (Kernel Address Space Layout Randomisation) -- kernel base randomised at boot; defeated by: leaking a kernel pointer via an info leak vulnerability (/proc/kallsyms if readable, side-channel timing attacks, or the vulnerability itself leaking a pointer). (3) SMEP (Supervisor Mode Execution Prevention) -- prevents kernel from executing userland code; defeated by ROP chains using kernel gadgets. SMAP (Supervisor Mode Access Prevention) -- prevents kernel from accessing userland data without explicit STAC/CLAC; defeats passing shellcode pointers in userland memory. (4) Kernel ROP -- chain kernel gadgets (find with ROPgadget --binary vmlinux); pivot to kernel stack; execute: commit_creds(prepare_kernel_cred(0)) to gain root. (5) Context -- after exploitation, return cleanly to userland (swapgs; iretq or SYSRET); crash-free return is essential as a kernel panic is detectable and takes down the system. (6) Exploit reliability -- kernel has no ASLR variance between processes but does have between boots; one-shot exploit must work reliably. Tools: syzkaller (kernel fuzzer), pwndbg for kernel debugging via QEMU."),
("Explain Windows kernel exploitation and Driver exploitation basics.", "Windows kernel exploitation: (1) Attack surface -- kernel drivers are a primary attack surface; IOCTLs (I/O Control codes) are handled by driver dispatch routines; user mode sends IOCTL via DeviceIoControl to a driver device object; if the driver doesn't validate input size or content, it's exploitable. (2) Finding vulnerabilities -- WinDBG with kernel debugging (2-machine setup or QEMU); IDA Pro / Ghidra to reverse driver binaries; IOCTL fuzzing (Driver Fuzzing Framework, ioct fuzzer). (3) Common vulnerability classes -- stack overflow in IOCTL handler; arbitrary kernel memory read/write; use-after-free in driver object management; type confusion in IOCTL dispatch. (4) Exploitation primitives -- arbitrary kernel write: overwrite HalDispatchTable+0x8 (HalpQuerySystemInformation) -- a common target; call NtQueryIntervalProfile in userland which calls through HalDispatchTable; this executes attacker's shellcode. Alternative: overwrite token of current process (SYSTEM token steal): shellcode walks EPROCESS list, finds SYSTEM process (PID 4), copies its token to current process's EPROCESS.Token. (5) BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) -- if kernel exploit requires vulnerable driver, attackers load a legitimate signed-but-vulnerable driver (e.g., AV drivers, hardware vendor drivers with CVEs) to gain kernel code execution; then use it as a stepping stone. Msi Afterburner's RTCore64.sys, Intel's iqvw64e.sys are known BYOVD targets. (6) Mitigations -- HVCI (Hypervisor-Protected Code Integrity) prevents loading of unsigned or maliciously signed code in kernel via VTL1 enforcement; DSE (Driver Signature Enforcement) blocks unsigned drivers; bypass requires a signed driver with a vulnerability."),

# ── REVERSE ENGINEERING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
("How do you reverse engineer a malware sample systematically?", "Systematic malware reverse engineering: (1) Safe environment -- execute and analyse only in an isolated VM (FlareVM on Windows, REMnux on Linux); snapshot before execution; disable network or use FakeNet-NG to simulate network responses and capture C2 traffic. (2) Static analysis (pre-execution): file type and hash (file, md5sum, VirusTotal submission); strings extraction (strings -a; FLOSS for decoded strings including stack strings and encoded strings); PE analysis (PE-bear, pestudio): imports (DLL/function names reveal capability -- CreateRemoteThread = injection, CryptEncrypt = ransomware, InternetOpenA = network C2), exports, sections (high entropy section = packed/encrypted code), compile timestamp, certificate. Detect packing: UPX, Themida, MPRESS; unpack with upx -d or manually. (3) Dynamic analysis (execution): Process Monitor (file/registry/network activity), Process Hacker (running processes, loaded DLLs, handles), Wireshark/FakeNet-NG (C2 protocol), Autoruns (persistence added), x64dbg with ScyllaHide (anti-debug bypass). (4) Code analysis (IDA Pro / Ghidra / Binary Ninja): start at entrypoint; rename functions as purpose becomes clear; identify crypto constants (find crypt search in IDA: AES S-box, RC4 PRNG, XOR keys); trace the main execution path; identify C2 communication function (connect, send, recv patterns). (5) Deobfuscation -- XOR decode with identified key; base64 decode; RC4 decrypt C2 config (IDA scripting or CyberChef). (6) Report -- IOCs (hashes, IPs, domains, mutex names, registry keys, file paths); capabilities mapped to MITRE ATT&CK; YARA rule from unique byte sequences."),
("How do you write a YARA rule for a new malware family?", "YARA rule writing process: (1) Collect samples -- get 3-5 samples of the malware family to find consistent artefacts; check: are they from the same family (VirusTotal/Intezer clustering)? (2) Extract unique strings -- run FLOSS on all samples; look for strings that appear in ALL samples (family-consistent) but NOT in benign software (low false positives). Candidate string types: C2 URLs/domains (most specific); mutex names (unique per family: 'Global\\WindowsUpdateMutex327842'); User-Agent strings; error messages or log strings embedded by the developer; encoded configuration markers. (3) Extract unique byte sequences -- in IDA, find characteristic code: crypto implementations, obfuscation routines, specific API call sequences; export as hex; verify across samples. (4) Test for false positives -- run candidate rule against: clean Windows install, common software corpus (VirusTotal Livehunt or a local corpus). Strings in common libraries (MSVC runtime, Windows API) generate false positives. (5) Write the rule:\n```yara\nrule Malware_FamilyX_v1 {\n    meta:\n        description = \"Detects FamilyX based on C2 config and mutex\"\n        author = \"Researcher\"\n        date = \"2025-01-01\"\n        reference = \"SHA256:abc123\"\n        mitre_attack = \"T1071.001\"\n    strings:\n        $mutex = \"Global\\\\WinUpdate3278\" ascii wide\n        $c2_cfg = { 68 74 74 70 73 3A 2F 2F } // https:// in config\n        $crypto_routine = { 31 C0 89 45 ?? 8B 45 ?? XOR ?? [2] 83 C0 01 }\n    condition:\n        uint16(0) == 0x5A4D and  // PE file\n        ($mutex and $c2_cfg) or\n        $crypto_routine\n}\n```\n(6) Tune threshold -- if false positives found, make condition more restrictive (require more strings, check PE characteristics, check entropy of specific sections). Deploy to: VirusTotal Livehunt, EDR YARA scanning, Elastic, Splunk."),

